.TH VM_PAGE 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
vm_page \- physical page management and replacement policy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/vm/vm_page.h>
.LP
.BI "vm_page_t *vm_page_alloc(vm_object_t *" object ", uint64_t " pindex ", int " req ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_page_free(vm_page_t *" m ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_page_activate(vm_page_t *" m ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_page_deactivate(vm_page_t *" m ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_page_wire(vm_page_t *" m ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_page_unwire(vm_page_t *" m ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_pageout_scan(int " max_scan ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_page_launder(vm_page_t *" m ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B vm_page
subsystem manages physical pages of memory, tracking their state, ownership, and position in the LRU queues.
.SS Page States
.IP \[bu] 2
.B PG_ACTIVE
\- Recently accessed pages. Scanned by the page daemon using a Clock algorithm.
.IP \[bu]
.B PG_INACTIVE
\- Candidates for reclamation. If clean, can be freed immediately. If dirty, must be laundered.
.IP \[bu]
.B PG_BUSY
\- Page is currently involved in an I/O operation or allocation.
.IP \[bu]
.B PG_DIRTY
\- Page contents have been modified and differ from backing store.
.SS Replacement Policy
The system uses a 
.B two-handed clock algorithm
(or approximate LRU). 
.B vm_pageout_scan
walks the active queue. Pages with the hardware reference bit set are given a "second chance" and moved to the tail of the active queue. Unreferenced pages are demoted to the inactive queue.
.PP
The
.B pageout daemon
(vm_pageout) wakes when free memory is low. It scans the active queue to refill the inactive queue, then reclaims clean inactive pages or launders dirty ones via the
.B vm_pager
interface.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vm_object (9),
.BR vm_pager (9),
.BR pmap (9)
